  "executive_briefing": "The global corporate tax environment in mid-2026 is defined by a retreat from multilateralism toward 'strategic domesticism.' While the OECD Pillar Two framework remains a technical reference point, its practical application is bifurcated by national interest, notably through the U.S. 14% OBBBA rate and sustained resistance from Central European states like Poland and Hungary. Trade policy has entered a 'post-IEEPA' era; following the February 2026 Supreme Court ruling, the U.S. executive branch has pivoted to aggressive but narrower Section 232 justifications for tariffs on metals and pharmaceuticals, creating a more litigious but legally bounded trade environment. Simultaneously, tax authorities in the ASEAN and MENA regions are accelerating the transition to real-time compliance, with Singapore\u2019s InvoiceNow mandate and new July 2026 e-invoicing pilots signaling the definitive end of retrospective filing for multinational entities.",
